## Proof

**Setup.** Let $V = \{p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4, p_5\}$ be the set of 5 propositional variables. A **valuation** is a function $v: V \to \{T, F\}$. There are $2^5 = 32$ valuations in total.

**Equivalence classes ↔ Boolean functions.** A propositional term $\varphi$ built from $V$ induces a truth function $f_\varphi: \{0,1\}^5 \to \{0,1\}$ mapping each valuation to the truth value of $\varphi$ under it. Two terms $\varphi, \psi$ are **logically equivalent** ($\varphi \equiv \psi$) iff $f_\varphi = f_\psi$, i.e., iff they are satisfied by exactly the same set of valuations. Thus equivalence classes of propositional terms are in bijection with Boolean functions on 5 variables, equivalently with subsets $S \subseteq \{0,1\}^5$ (the satisfying set $S_\varphi = \{v : f_\varphi(v) = 1\}$).

**Counting the target classes.** A term evaluates to TRUE under exactly 13 valuations iff its satisfying set $S_\varphi$ has cardinality 13. The number of such equivalence classes equals the number of 13-element subsets of the 32-element set of valuations:

$$\binom{32}{13}.$$

**Computation.** Using the recurrence $\binom{32}{k} = \binom{32}{k-1}\cdot\frac{33-k}{k}$:

| $k$ | $\binom{32}{k}$ |
|-----|------------------|
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 32 |
| 2 | 496 |
| 3 | 4,960 |
| 4 | 35,960 |
| 5 | 201,376 |
| 6 | 906,192 |
| 7 | 3,365,856 |
| 8 | 10,518,300 |
| 9 | 28,048,800 |
| 10 | 64,512,240 |
| 11 | 129,024,480 |
| 12 | 225,792,840 |
| 13 | 347,373,600 |

The last step: $\binom{32}{13} = \binom{32}{12}\cdot\frac{20}{13} = 225{,}792{,}840 \times \frac{20}{13} = \frac{4{,}515{,}856{,}800}{13} = 347{,}373{,}600$.

Verification: $347{,}373{,}600 \times 13 = 4{,}515{,}856{,}800$. ✓

**Answer.** The number of equivalence classes of propositional terms over 5 variables that are true under exactly 13 valuations is

$$\boxed{\binom{32}{13} = 347{,}373{,}600}.$$

